---
/**
 * Makes date-filtered pages correct themselves in the reader's browser.
 *
 * Everything on these sites that says "upcoming" is decided when the page is
 * built. A scheduled rebuild keeps that honest (see
 * .github/workflows/scheduled-rebuild.yml) but a rebuild can fail, and GitHub
 * switches scheduled workflows off by itself after sixty days without a push —
 * silently, in exactly the quiet winter when nobody would notice.
 *
 * So the pages do not depend on it. Every event carries the last day it is on
 * and, as `data-end`, the instant it comes off the page (`listedUntil`: its
 * end time, or midnight when it gave none), and anything finished is removed
 * on load. The day alone kept a 7 pm talk up until midnight; the instant is
 * the check the hub's weekend page already made. A build that is a week old
 * then shows fewer events rather than wrong ones, which is the difference
 * between a stale page and a lying one.
 *
 * The end decides on its own; the day is only read when a row has no usable
 * end. Or-ing the two took an overnight show down at midnight while it was
 * still on (follow-up audit, 8 October 2026). The rule and the pruning that
 * follows it (day headings, jump links, empty states rebuilt from what
 * survives) are src/lib/expiry.ts, so they can be replayed in a test.
 */
---
<script>
  import { pruneExpired } from '@/lib/expiry';

  // The sites keep their dates as Denver wall-clock days, so ask for the date
  // in Denver rather than wherever the reader happens to be. The rule itself
  // (a known end decides; the day only stands in when there is none) lives in
  // src/lib/expiry.ts, where the clock cases are tested.
  const today = new Date().toLocaleDateString('en-CA', { timeZone: 'America/Denver' });
  pruneExpired(document.body, Date.now(), today);
</script>
